(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-2-pd1-exam2-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"PD1 Exam 2 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form B","category":"Physical Diagnosis","questions":[{"q":"Normal oral body temperature is approximately 37°C (98.6°F), and may vary diurnally by about 1 degree, being...","choices":["constant throughout the entire day with no variation","highest at midnight specifically","lowest in the morning and highest in the evening","highest in the morning and lowest in the evening"],"answer":2,"exp":"Body temperature normally follows a diurnal pattern, being lowest in the early morning (AM) and highest in the evening (PM), with normal variation of about 1 degree across the day. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"The closure of the AV (atrioventricular) valves (mitral and tricuspid) at the start of systole generates which heart sound?","choices":["S1","S2","S4","S3"],"answer":0,"exp":"S1 is generated by closure of the AV (atrioventricular) valves (mitral and tricuspid) marking the start of systole. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"When inserting the otoscope into the ear canal, what technique should the examiner use?","choices":["Insert the speculum quickly and forcefully to minimize the patient's overall discomfort","Hold the otoscope loosely without bracing the hand against the patient's head","Angle the otoscope tip upward and backward, away from the ear canal floor","Brace the hand against the patient's head, insert gently, and angle down and forward"],"answer":3,"exp":"Bracing the hand against the head prevents injury from sudden patient movement, and the otoscope should be inserted gently, angled down and forward. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"During the DRE (digital rectal examination), after the patient squeezes the anal sphincter, the examiner proceeds to palpate the rectal surface, systematically assessing for...","choices":["heart murmurs, diastolic gallops, and rubs on cardiac auscultation","thyroid nodules and overall gland size on neck palpation","lung crackles and wheezes heard on pulmonary auscultation","masses, tenderness, and irregularities of the rectal mucosa"],"answer":3,"exp":"Systematic palpation of the rectal surface during the DRE (digital rectal examination) screens for masses, tenderness, and mucosal irregularities that might indicate polyps, tumors, or other pathology. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"Transillumination, one of the special techniques for assessing scrotal contents, is most useful for distinguishing...","choices":["a fluid-filled mass (which transilluminates/glows) from a solid mass (which does not)","left-sided testicular size from right-sided testicular size differences alone","normal urine coloration from abnormal, cloudy discoloration seen in the urine","arterial blood flow from venous blood flow within the spermatic cord itself"],"answer":0,"exp":"Transillumination involves shining a light source through the scrotal skin; a fluid-filled structure like a hydrocele will transilluminate (glow red/orange), while a solid mass (such as a tumor) will not, helping distinguish between these possibilities. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"When documenting bowel sound findings, the four possible descriptive categories are...","choices":["mild, moderate, severe, critical","absent, normoactive, hyperactive, hypoactive","only present or absent, with no other categories","loud, soft, medium, silent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Bowel sound findings are documented using one of four standard categories: absent, normoactive, hyperactive, or hypoactive. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"During chest expansion assessment, the examiner specifically watches for...","choices":["the patient's heart rate on the cardiac monitor, exclusively","changes in the patient's overlying skin color, exclusively","the size and reactivity of the patient's pupils, exclusively","the distance between the thumbs as the patient inhales deeply"],"answer":3,"exp":"As the patient takes a deep breath, the examiner watches the thumbs (positioned near the spine) move apart symmetrically; the distance they separate reflects the degree of chest expansion, and asymmetry can indicate unilateral lung pathology. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"Bilirubin, a yellow-brown pigment that can affect skin color, is released into circulation from...","choices":["prolonged sun exposure exclusively, with no other source","direct secretion from active sebaceous glands","the ongoing synthesis of new melanin pigment","the breakdown of heme molecules from red blood cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Bilirubin is a yellow-brown pigment released into the circulation as a breakdown product of heme (from red blood cell turnover); elevated levels can cause jaundice, a yellowing of the skin. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"In the visual acuity notation 20/40, the top number (20) represents the distance from the chart, and the bottom number (40) represents...","choices":["the diameter of the patient's pupil, as measured in millimeters during testing","the total number of letters the patient missed while reading that line","the patient's age in years at the time the acuity test was performed","the distance at which a person with normal vision could read that same line"],"answer":3,"exp":"In 20/40 notation, the top number is the testing distance (20 feet), and the bottom number is the distance at which a person with normal visual acuity could still read that same line (40 feet) — so 20/40 means the patient must be at 20 feet to read what a person with normal vision could read from 40 feet away, indicating below-normal acuity. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 18"},{"q":"Compared with oral temperature, rectal temperature readings are typically...","choices":["0.4-0.5°C higher","identical to oral temperature in all cases","2-3°C higher","0.4-0.5°C lower"],"answer":0,"exp":"Rectal temperature readings are typically 0.4-0.5°C higher than oral readings, while axillary temperatures run about 1°C lower than oral, and tympanic readings are more variable. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"The closure of the semilunar valves (aortic and pulmonic) at the end of ventricular contraction generates which heart sound?","choices":["S1","S3","S2","S4"],"answer":2,"exp":"S2 is generated by closure of the semilunar valves, marking the end of systole and the start of diastole. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"During the whispered voice test, how should the examiner position and prepare the patient?","choices":["Stand 2 feet behind the patient, occlude the non-tested ear, and rub the tragus","Have the patient close their eyes but leave both ears completely unoccluded","Stand directly in front of the patient at approximately an arm's length","Perform the test only in a completely silent room, using no masking maneuver"],"answer":0,"exp":"The examiner stands 2 feet behind the patient, occludes the ear not being tested, and rubs the tragus to mask sound to that ear. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"During the prostatic examination (performed via the rectal approach), the examiner assesses the prostate for...","choices":["size, symmetry, consistency, and the presence of nodules or tenderness","bowel sound frequency, assessed by abdominal auscultation instead","blood pressure and heart rate only, as routine vital signs","skin turgor, assessed elsewhere as a marker of hydration status"],"answer":0,"exp":"The digital prostate exam evaluates the gland's size, symmetry (comparing the two lateral lobes), consistency (should be smooth and rubbery, not stony-hard), and checks for any nodules or areas of tenderness. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"Regarding prostate cancer health promotion and counseling, which statement is accurate?","choices":["It is diagnosed with roughly equal frequency in men younger than forty and men considerably older","It is one of the most frequently diagnosed cancers in US men, rare before age 40, with higher incidence in African American men","It has a distinctly lower incidence in African American men than in other racial groups nationally","It is one of the least frequently diagnosed cancers among men anywhere in the United States today"],"answer":1,"exp":"Prostate cancer is among the most frequently diagnosed cancers in US men; it is rare before age 40, and epidemiologic data show a higher incidence in African American men, all relevant points for health promotion/counseling discussions. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"When listening for vascular bruits over the aorta, renal, iliac, and femoral arteries, the examiner should use...","choices":["the diaphragm of the stethoscope","the stethoscope tubing pressed directly on skin","no stethoscope at all, only palpation","the bell of the stethoscope"],"answer":0,"exp":"Bruits are best heard using the diaphragm of the stethoscope over the aorta, renal arteries, iliac arteries, and femoral arteries. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 21"},{"q":"Vesicular breath sounds, one of the three normal breath sound types, are characterized by...","choices":["equal inspiratory and expiratory sounds, of intermediate intensity and pitch throughout","complete absence of any audible sound throughout the entire respiratory breathing cycle","expiratory sounds lasting longer than inspiratory sounds, loud and high-pitched throughout","inspiratory sounds lasting longer than expiratory sounds, soft in intensity and low in pitch"],"answer":3,"exp":"Vesicular breath sounds — heard over most of the normal lung fields — have inspiratory sounds that last longer than expiratory sounds, and are soft in intensity and low in pitch. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 49"},{"q":"Sebaceous glands, which secrete a fatty substance onto the skin via hair follicles, are present on all skin surfaces except...","choices":["the entire body, with no exceptions","the scalp","the face","the palms and soles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Sebaceous glands are present on essentially all skin surfaces except the palms and soles, which lack hair follicles (through which sebaceous glands normally secrete). — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"When recording visual acuity results, the examiner should record the smallest line of print where...","choices":["more than half of the letters are correctly identified","the patient guesses randomly with no correct answers required","every single letter is read with zero errors","fewer than a quarter of letters are correctly identified"],"answer":0,"exp":"The recorded visual acuity line is the smallest line where the patient correctly identifies more than half of the letters, not necessarily every single letter perfectly. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 18"},{"q":"Compared with oral temperature, axillary temperature readings are typically...","choices":["about 1°C lower","always inaccurate and never used","about 1°C higher","identical to rectal temperature"],"answer":0,"exp":"Axillary temperature readings typically run about 1°C lower than oral readings, while rectal readings run 0.4-0.5°C higher than oral. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"During systole on the left side of the heart, which valve is open and which is closed?","choices":["Aortic valve open; mitral valve closed","Aortic valve closed; mitral valve open","Both aortic and mitral valves closed","Both aortic and mitral valves open"],"answer":0,"exp":"During systole the aortic valve opens to allow ejection into the aorta while the mitral valve closes to prevent regurgitation into the left atrium. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"During the whispered voice test, what constitutes a normal result?","choices":["The patient correctly repeats 6 out of 6 whispered letters/numbers","The patient correctly repeats 1 out of 6 whispered letters/numbers","The patient correctly repeats 3 out of 6 whispered letters/numbers","There is no defined scoring threshold for this test"],"answer":2,"exp":"Normal is defined as 3 out of 6 items correctly repeated (note: the lecture flags 4/6 as an abnormal result to highlight care in scoring). — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"An anal fissure classically presents with which typical location and symptom pattern?","choices":["A firm, non-tender nodule palpable only on DRE (digital rectal examination)","A tear most often in the posterior midline, causing sharp pain with defecation","A painless mass located laterally, entirely unrelated to defecation timing","Diffuse perianal itching present continuously with no visible lesion at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Anal fissures classically occur in the posterior midline (due to relatively reduced blood supply there) and cause sharp, tearing pain during and after defecation, often with a small amount of bright red bleeding. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slides 7, 11"},{"q":"PSA (prostate-specific antigen) is referenced as a tool for...","choices":["diagnosing erectile dysfunction","screening for testicular cancer","assessing kidney function","screening for prostate cancer"],"answer":3,"exp":"PSA (prostate-specific antigen) is specifically referenced as a screening test used in the context of prostate cancer health promotion and counseling. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"A friction rub heard over the liver or spleen is a rare finding that indicates...","choices":["significant systemic dehydration and volume depletion","a completely normal vascular sound heard at rest in everyone","entirely normal, healthy bowel peristaltic activity","inflammation of the peritoneal surface of that organ"],"answer":3,"exp":"A friction rub is a grating sound heard with respiration over the liver or spleen, indicating inflammation of that organ's peritoneal surface — a rare but clinically significant finding. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"Bronchovesicular breath sounds are characterized by...","choices":["a description with essentially no defined or reproducible acoustic characteristics at all","expiratory sounds much longer than inspiratory sounds, loud and high-pitched throughout","inspiratory sounds much longer than expiratory sounds, soft and low-pitched throughout","inspiratory and expiratory sounds that are roughly equal in length, of intermediate intensity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Bronchovesicular breath sounds have inspiratory and expiratory phases that are approximately equal in duration and intermediate in intensity, typically heard over the main bronchi/central airways. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 49"},{"q":"Apocrine sweat glands, distinct from the more widely distributed eccrine glands, are primarily located in the...","choices":["axillary and groin regions, opening primarily into hair follicles","everywhere on the body, identical in distribution to eccrine glands","the palms and soles exclusively, opening directly onto skin","the scalp exclusively, opening directly onto the skin surface"],"answer":0,"exp":"Apocrine glands are primarily located in the axillary and groin regions and open primarily into hair follicles, in contrast to eccrine glands, which are widely distributed and open directly onto the skin surface. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"Visual acuity testing should always be performed...","choices":["before any tests that direct light at the eyes","after the ophthalmoscopic exam, and never before it","only if the patient specifically requests it be done","in any order, since sequence does not matter at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"Visual acuity should always be tested before any subsequent tests that involve shining light into the eyes (such as pupillary light reaction or ophthalmoscopy), since those tests can transiently affect the patient's vision/comfort. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 18"},{"q":"When taking an oral temperature, the thermometer should be placed...","choices":["between the cheek and the gums, with the mouth held open the entire time","anywhere convenient in the mouth, since the exact placement does not matter at all","on top of the tongue, near the front teeth, with the mouth left open","under the tongue, along the lingual frenulum, as far back as possible, with the mouth closed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Proper oral temperature technique places the thermometer under the tongue along the lingual frenulum, positioned as far back as possible, with the patient's mouth closed around it for an accurate reading. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"Pulse pressure is defined as:","choices":["The peak arterial pressure generated during ventricular systole","The pressure drop measured across a stenotic aortic valve","The difference between systolic and diastolic pressure","The mean arterial pressure averaged across the cardiac cycle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Pulse pressure is the difference between the highest (systolic) and lowest (diastolic) arterial pressures. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 16"},{"q":"If an abnormality is found on hearing screening, which further tests help distinguish conductive from sensorineural hearing loss?","choices":["Otoscopic examination alone","Weber and Rinne tuning fork tests","Transillumination and palpation","Whispered voice test repeated a second time"],"answer":1,"exp":"The Weber and Rinne tests are used to further evaluate and distinguish conductive versus sensorineural hearing loss. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"Internal hemorrhoids differ from external hemorrhoids primarily in that internal hemorrhoids...","choices":["arise above the dentate (pectinate) line and are generally not palpable/visible unless prolapsed","require no special positioning or additional instrumentation whatsoever to detect","are always significantly more painful than external hemorrhoids upon direct palpation","are located exclusively on the visible external perianal skin surface, never internally"],"answer":0,"exp":"Internal hemorrhoids originate above the dentate line, in an area with visceral innervation, so they are typically painless and not visible/palpable unless they prolapse; external hemorrhoids arise below the dentate line, in somatically innervated skin, and are often visible and can be quite painful. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 11"},{"q":"Performing a testicular self-examination, one of the special techniques taught to patients, is best done...","choices":["monthly, ideally after a warm shower when the scrotal skin is relaxed","only during a cold shower, when the scrotal skin is taut","only a single time, during the patient's very first urology visit","only by a clinician annually, never performed by the patient"],"answer":0,"exp":"Testicular self-exam is typically recommended on a monthly basis, ideally after a warm shower when the scrotal skin is relaxed, making it easier to detect any new masses or changes. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"A venous hum, heard over the epigastrium or umbilicus as a soft humming sound in both systole and diastole, indicates...","choices":["acute appendicitis with localized peritoneal inflammation and marked rebound tenderness","increased collateral circulation between the portal and systemic venous systems, as in hepatic cirrhosis","entirely normal peristaltic bowel activity heard consistently throughout the healthy abdomen","a completely normal vascular finding expected to be present in all healthy, unaffected adults"],"answer":1,"exp":"A venous hum reflects increased collateral blood flow between the portal and systemic venous systems, classically associated with hepatic cirrhosis and portal hypertension. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"Bronchial (tracheal/tubular) breath sounds are characterized by...","choices":["a description that does not apply anywhere within a normal, healthy adult chest exam","equal inspiratory and expiratory sounds of intermediate intensity throughout the cycle","expiratory sounds lasting longer than inspiratory sounds, loud in intensity and high in pitch","inspiratory sounds lasting longer than expiratory sounds, soft in intensity and low in pitch"],"answer":2,"exp":"Bronchial (tubular) breath sounds — normally heard only over the trachea/large airways — have expiratory sounds lasting longer than inspiratory sounds, and are loud and high-pitched; hearing this sound pattern over peripheral lung fields is abnormal and suggests consolidation. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 49"},{"q":"Vellus hair, one of the two hair types, is best described as...","choices":["found exclusively on the scalp and eyebrows","coarse and heavily pigmented, like scalp hair","short, fine, and less pigmented ('peach fuzz')","entirely absent in all healthy individuals"],"answer":2,"exp":"Vellus hair is short, fine, and lightly pigmented — informally called 'peach fuzz' — in contrast to terminal hair, which is coarser and more heavily pigmented (for example, scalp hair, eyebrows). — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"Visual field testing by confrontation serves as a...","choices":["test of pupillary reaction to both direct and consensual light","test of hearing function, unrelated to the visual pathway","screening test for lesions in the anterior and posterior visual pathway","definitive diagnostic test that requires no further workup"],"answer":2,"exp":"Confrontation visual field testing is a bedside screening test for lesions along the visual pathway; a definitive diagnosis of a visual field defect requires automated perimetry testing performed by an ophthalmologist. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 19"},{"q":"For rectal temperature measurement, the lubricated thermometer tip should be inserted into the anal canal to a depth of approximately...","choices":["15-20 cm","there is no specific insertion depth","3-4 cm","0.5 cm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Rectal temperature technique calls for inserting the lubricated thermometer tip approximately 3-4 cm into the anal canal for an accurate core-temperature-adjacent reading. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"For a blood pressure of 120/80 mm Hg, what is the pulse pressure, and is it within the normal range?","choices":["80 mm Hg, which is abnormally high for this reading","40 mm Hg, which is within the normal 30–40 mm Hg range","120 mm Hg, which is abnormally elevated for this reading","20 mm Hg, which is abnormally low for this patient"],"answer":1,"exp":"120 − 80 = 40 mm Hg, which falls within the normal pulse pressure range of 30–40 mm Hg. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 16"},{"q":"On nasal inspection, which findings should be assessed?","choices":["Only the distribution and density of visible nasal hair within each nostril, with no other structural findings recorded","Only the presence or absence of a visibly deviated nasal septum, with no other mucosal findings recorded at all","Deformities, asymmetry, polyps, nares/turbinates, mucosa color/swelling/bleeding, exudate character, and septal deviation/inflammation/perforation","Only tenderness elicited on direct palpation over the frontal and maxillary sinuses, with no other findings assessed"],"answer":2,"exp":"These are the comprehensive nasal inspection findings listed in the lecture. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 34"},{"q":"A key clinical use of the DRE (digital rectal examination), beyond palpating the rectal wall and prostate, is to...","choices":["directly visualize the entire sigmoid colon through the anal canal","obtain a stool sample from the examining glove to test for occult blood","assess symmetric chest wall expansion during quiet respiration","measure blood pressure directly from within the rectal wall itself"],"answer":1,"exp":"A standard part of the DRE (digital rectal examination) is checking any stool present on the examining glove for occult (hidden) blood, which can be an important screening step for GI (gastrointestinal) bleeding even when hematochezia is not grossly visible. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"Examining the spermatic cord, part of the standard male GU (genitourinary) exam sequence, is performed by...","choices":["palpating along its length from the testis up to the external inguinal ring","measuring blood pressure directly over the femoral pulse in the groin","percussing gently over the scrotal skin with a plexor finger","auscultating for bowel sounds transmitted down into the groin region"],"answer":0,"exp":"The spermatic cord is examined by palpating along its length, from the testis up toward the external inguinal ring, assessing for masses, tenderness, or thickening (which could suggest a varicocele or other pathology). — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"During abdominal percussion, tympany typically predominates because of the presence of...","choices":["dense muscle tissue","fluid collections exclusively","solid organs exclusively","gas within hollow organs"],"answer":3,"exp":"Tympany (a hollow, drum-like percussion note) predominates over most of the abdomen because gas within the hollow GI (gastrointestinal) organs is the most common underlying content, in contrast to dullness, which occurs over fluid, feces, or solid organs. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"Adventitious breath sounds — abnormal sounds superimposed on the normal breath sounds — include rales (crackles), rhonchi, wheezes, and...","choices":["bronchial sounds heard appropriately over the trachea","bronchovesicular sounds","friction rubs","vesicular sounds"],"answer":2,"exp":"The adventitious breath sounds listed include rales (crackles), rhonchi, wheezes, and friction rubs (plus the less common amphoric sounds), all of which represent abnormal findings superimposed on the normal breath sound pattern. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 51"},{"q":"The lunula, a nail structure, is best described as...","choices":["the tissue protecting the space between the proximal nail fold and nail plate","the tissue protecting the sides of the nail plate","the white, half-moon shape at the proximal nail bed","the colored portion of the nail plate reflecting the vasculature of the nail bed"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lunula is the whitish, half-moon-shaped area visible at the proximal end of the nail bed, a normal nail structure. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 11"},{"q":"During the static finger wiggle test for monocular visual fields, the examiner brings a wiggling finger slowly forward from...","choices":["a position equidistant from the examiner and patient's uncovered eyes, into the center of view until seen","a position directly behind the patient's head, so it never enters the visual field at all","a position directly touching the surface of the patient's open eye at the very start","a position directly in front of the patient's nose only, without ever moving laterally into view"],"answer":0,"exp":"The examiner starts with the hand equidistant from both the examiner's and patient's uncovered eyes and slowly brings the wiggling finger forward into the center of the visual field until the patient reports seeing it, testing each quadrant this way. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"For tympanic temperature measurement to be accurate, the ear canal must be clear to the tympanic membrane, and the infrared beam must...","choices":["never make contact with the ear canal","be aimed completely away from the ear","aim only at the outer ear canal wall","aim directly at the tympanic membrane"],"answer":3,"exp":"Accurate tympanic temperature measurement requires the ear canal to be clear (for example, no significant cerumen obstruction) and the infrared beam to be properly aimed directly at the tympanic membrane. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 28"},{"q":"On the Littmann Cardiology IV stethoscope, the bell side (light pressure) is best used for which findings?","choices":["Heart murmurs, S3/S4 heart sounds, and bruits","Only carotid bruits","Normal heartbeats, breath sounds, and bowel sounds","Only pediatric heart sounds"],"answer":0,"exp":"The bell, used with light pressure, best transmits low-frequency sounds such as murmurs, S3/S4, and bruits. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 17"},{"q":"When using the otoscope to examine the nose, what technique should be used?","choices":["Use the smallest available speculum, tilt the patient's head forward, and press directly on the septum","Hold the otoscope handle straight up and insert the tip directly against the septum itself","Use the largest speculum, tilt the patient's head back, hold the handle to the side, and avoid the septum","No speculum attachment is needed at all for a proper nasal examination, since the naked eye is sufficient"],"answer":2,"exp":"This is the correct technique for otoscopic nasal examination as described in the lecture. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"Besides the standard left-lateral position, an alternative position sometimes used for the anorectal exam (particularly convenient when combined with a pelvic exam) is the...","choices":["lithotomy position","standing on one leg","Trendelenburg position","reverse Trendelenburg position"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lithotomy position, already used for pelvic examination, can also conveniently accommodate an anorectal exam in the same setup, complementing the standard left-lateral position used more generally. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 'bag of worms' texture palpated above the testis, classically more common on the left side, is the description associated with...","choices":["a hydrocele","a varicocele","a normal testis","testicular torsion"],"answer":1,"exp":"A varicocele — dilated, tortuous veins of the pampiniform plexus — classically produces a 'bag of worms' texture on palpation above the testis, and is more common on the left side due to venous drainage anatomy (left testicular vein draining into the left renal vein at a less favorable angle). — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"If an area of unexpected dullness is discovered during abdominal percussion, the examiner should...","choices":["ignore it entirely, since dullness is always considered a normal exam finding","percuss around the area to define its extent, considering an underlying mass or enlarged organ","immediately stop the entire abdominal exam and refer the patient onward for imaging","switch immediately to auscultation only, abandoning further percussion technique"],"answer":1,"exp":"Unexpected dullness should prompt the examiner to percuss around the area to map its borders/extent, helping determine whether it reflects an underlying mass or organomegaly. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"Bronchophony is assessed by having the patient say 'ninety-nine' while the examiner auscultates; a positive finding (louder, clearer transmission) suggests...","choices":["underlying lung consolidation","a completely normal lung finding","normal vesicular breath sounds","pneumothorax exclusively"],"answer":0,"exp":"Increased, clearer transmission of the spoken voice (bronchophony) occurs when underlying lung tissue is consolidated (for example, from pneumonia), since consolidated tissue transmits sound more efficiently than normal aerated lung. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 52"},{"q":"The nail plate normally gets its color from the vasculature of the nail bed and should appear as a variation of...","choices":["pink","deep blue at baseline","pure white with no variation","bright yellow"],"answer":0,"exp":"The nail plate's color derives from the underlying nail bed vasculature and should normally appear as some variation of pink, though shape and opacity can vary, and pigment bands may be a normal finding in darker-skinned individuals. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 11"},{"q":"During monocular visual field confrontation testing, an important assumption underlying the test is that...","choices":["the examiner has no visual field deficits of their own","the patient has no visual field deficits of their own","no equipment or technique assumptions are made at all","both of the patient's eyes must remain open throughout"],"answer":0,"exp":"Because the examiner's own visual field serves as the comparison standard for detecting the patient's deficits, the test specifically assumes the examiner has normal, intact visual fields. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"For temporal artery temperature measurement, the technique involves placing the probe on the center of the forehead and then...","choices":["moving it across the forehead, down the cheek, and behind the earlobe without lifting it","lifting and repositioning the probe several separate times before completing one reading","inserting the probe tip directly into the patient's external ear canal instead","holding it perfectly still in one single fixed spot on the forehead"],"answer":0,"exp":"Temporal artery thermometry technique involves sliding the probe continuously (without lifting) from the center of the forehead, across the forehead, down the cheek, and behind the earlobe. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed as a concerning cardiac symptom to ask about during history taking?","choices":["Myalgia (muscle ache)","Eye light sensitivity","Tinnitus (ear ringing)","Syncope (fainting)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Syncope is listed among the concerning cardiac symptoms to elicit in the history. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 19"},{"q":"When palpating the sinuses, how should the frontal sinuses be assessed?","choices":["Press down on the bridge of the nose","No palpation of sinuses is performed; only transillumination is used","Press up from under the brow ridge, avoiding the eyes","Press directly on the eyes for tenderness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Frontal sinus palpation involves pressing up from under the brow ridge while avoiding the eyes. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 36"},{"q":"Beyond hematochezia, other key elements of the anorectal health history include pain on defecation and...","choices":["visual field disturbances","anal and perianal lesions","chest pain on exertion","hearing loss or tinnitus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The anorectal health history specifically screens for hematochezia, pain on defecation, and anal/perianal lesions, giving a focused symptom set that guides the subsequent physical exam. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"A hydrocele is best described as...","choices":["a painless, fluid-filled swelling of the tunica vaginalis surrounding the testis","a normal anatomic finding present in essentially every male patient","a description of dilated, tortuous veins within the spermatic cord","a solid, tender mass that arises directly from the testis itself"],"answer":0,"exp":"A hydrocele is a painless collection of fluid within the tunica vaginalis surrounding the testis, producing a smooth scrotal swelling that classically transilluminates. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"To measure the vertical span of liver dullness in the right midclavicular line, the examiner starts percussing below the umbilicus and moves upward to find...","choices":["the upper border of liver dullness","the lower border of liver dullness","CVA (costovertebral angle) tenderness","the splenic percussion sign"],"answer":1,"exp":"Starting below the umbilicus and percussing upward (from tympany toward dullness) identifies the lower border of liver dullness, which is then combined with the separately-determined upper border to calculate liver span. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 26"}]};
